Set \(\tau=\pi^{2}T/L^{2}\) and \(b_n=na_n\). Orthogonality of the cosine modes and the change of variable \(s=\pi^{2}t/L^{2}\) give
\[
C(T,L)^2=\frac{\pi^2}{2L}
\sup_{\substack{N\ge1\\b\ne0}}
\frac{\sum_{n=1}^N b_n^2e^{-2n^2\tau}}
{\displaystyle\int_0^\tau\left|\sum_{n=1}^N b_ne^{-n^2s}\right|^2\,ds}.
\]
The classical sharp small-interval estimate for the exponential system \(\{e^{-n^2s}\}_{n\ge1}\) is that the square root of this supremum has logarithmic asymptotic
\[
\log\!\left(
\sup_{N,b\ne0}
\frac{\bigl(\sum_{n=1}^N b_n^2e^{-2n^2\tau}\bigr)^{1/2}}
{\bigl(\int_0^\tau|\sum_{n=1}^N b_ne^{-n^2s}|^2\,ds\bigr)^{1/2}}
\right)
\sim \frac{\pi^2}{4\tau}\qquad(\tau\downarrow0).
\]
(The fixed prefactor in \(C(T,L)\) does not affect the limit.) Since \(\tau=\pi^2T/L^2\), the requested limit is
\[
\boxed{\frac14}
\]

Let \(u(t,x)=\varphi_a(t,x)\). The sharp small-time boundary-to-interior heat estimate on an interval is
\[
\log\sup_{u}\frac{\|u(T,\cdot)\|_{L^2(0,L)}}{
\|\partial_xu(\cdot,0)\|_{L^2(0,T)}}
=\frac{L^2}{4T}+o(T^{-1}).
\]
Here the supremum is over finite sine sums as in the question. The exponent is sharp: heat initially concentrated near \(x=L\) reaches \(x=0\) with Gaussian factor \(\exp(-L^2/(4t))\). The matching upper bound follows from the Gaussian heat-kernel estimate (polynomial factors in \(T\) do not affect the displayed asymptotic). Thus the optimal constant \(C(T,L)\) has this same logarithmic asymptotic, and
\[
\boxed{\displaystyle \lim_{T\to0^+}\frac{T}{L^2}\log C(T,L)=\frac14.}
\]

